5. FUNCTION-BY-FUNCTION BREAKDOWN
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.1 void setup()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Runs once at power-on. Initializes hardware and note array state.

WHAT IT DOES:
  1. Sets buttonPin as INPUT_PULLUP
  2. Initializes the OLED display
  3. Clears the display buffer
  4. Seeds the random number generator (available for future use, though 
     current version doesn't use randomness in note timing/position - 
     notes always spawn at a fixed X position)
  5. Calls resetNotes() to ensure all note slots start inactive

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.2 void resetNotes()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Clears all note slots, marking them as inactive and resetting 
their position to the spawn point.

WHAT IT DOES:
  For each of the MAX_NOTES (6) slots:
    - noteActive[i] = false (slot available for new note)
    - noteY[i] = spawnY (reset position to top, though irrelevant while 
      inactive)

CALLED FROM: setup() (initial state) and startGame() (fresh game session)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.3 void loop()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Main game state machine using a switch statement (same 
architectural pattern as Memory Beep, but with only 3 states).

STRUCTURE:
  switch(state) {
    case START_SCREEN:    -> show title screen, wait for press to start
    case PLAYING:         -> call updateGame() + drawGame(), then delay(20)
    case GAME_OVER_STATE: -> show final results, wait for press to restart
  }

NOTE: Unlike Memory Beep's more complex phase-based state machine, 
Rhythm Tap's PLAYING state handles BOTH note movement AND player input 
simultaneously within a single continuous state - this reflects the 
real-time, continuous nature of rhythm games versus turn-based memory 
games.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.4 void startGame()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Resets all game variables and begins a fresh play session.

WHAT IT DOES:
  1. score = 0, level = 1
  2. combo = 0, maxCombo = 0, misses = 0
  3. noteSpeed reset to 1.2 (base speed)
  4. spawnInterval reset to 1200ms (base spawn rate)
  5. resetNotes() called (clears any leftover notes from previous game)
  6. lastSpawnTime = millis() (starts the spawn timer fresh)
  7. feedbackText = "" (clears any leftover feedback message)
  8. state = PLAYING (begins active gameplay)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.5 void spawnNote()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Activates one available (inactive) note slot to create a new 
falling note.

HOW IT WORKS:
  Loops through all MAX_NOTES slots, finds the FIRST inactive slot 
  (noteActive[i] == false), and:
    - Sets noteActive[i] = true
    - Resets noteY[i] = spawnY (starts at top of screen)
    - Breaks out of the loop immediately after activating one note 
      (only spawns ONE note per call)

DESIGN NOTE: This "object pooling" pattern (reusing a fixed-size array 
of slots rather than dynamically creating/destroying note objects) is a 
memory-efficient technique well-suited to Arduino's limited RAM - it 
avoids the need for dynamic memory allocation entirely.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.6 void updateGame()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Core game logic - handles note spawning, movement, missed-note 
detection, button input, feedback timing, and game-over conditions. 
Called once per frame from loop() during the PLAYING state.

SECTION-BY-SECTION BREAKDOWN:

  A) NOTE SPAWNING (TIME-BASED)
     if (now - lastSpawnTime > spawnInterval) {
       spawnNote();
       lastSpawnTime = now;
     }
     Checks if enough time has passed since the last spawn to trigger a 
     new note, using non-blocking millis()-based timing (consistent with 
     the pattern used throughout GameCraft games).

  B) NOTE MOVEMENT & MISS DETECTION (LOOP OVER ALL 6 SLOTS)
     For each ACTIVE note:
       - noteY[i] += noteSpeed (moves the note down by the current speed, 
         using float arithmetic for smooth sub-pixel movement)
       - IF noteY[i] > 64 (note has moved past the bottom of the screen 
         WITHOUT being tapped):
           - noteActive[i] = false (deactivate/remove the note)
           - registerMiss() called (counts as a miss, breaks combo)

  C) BUTTON INPUT (EDGE DETECTION)
     bool currentButtonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);
     if (currentButtonState == HIGH && lastButtonState == LOW) {
       handleTap();
     }
     lastButtonState = currentButtonState;
     
     Same edge-detection pattern as Stop The Bar - ensures handleTap() 
     fires exactly once per physical button press, regardless of hold 
     duration.

  D) FEEDBACK TEXT AUTO-CLEAR
     if (feedbackText != "" && now - feedbackTime > feedbackDuration) {
       feedbackText = "";
     }
     After 400ms, any currently displayed feedback message ("PERFECT!", 
     "MISS", etc.) automatically clears itself, keeping the display 
     clean for the next event.

  E) GAME OVER CHECK
     if (misses >= maxMisses) {
       if (score > bestScore) bestScore = score;
       state = GAME_OVER_STATE;
     }
     Checked every frame - as soon as the miss threshold (5) is reached, 
     the game immediately transitions to the game over state.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.7 void handleTap()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Called when the player presses the button. Determines WHICH 
note (if any) the player was attempting to hit, scores the attempt based 
on timing accuracy, and updates combo/difficulty progression.

LOGIC BREAKDOWN:

  A) FIND THE CLOSEST ACTIVE NOTE TO THE HIT ZONE
     Loops through all MAX_NOTES slots, and for each ACTIVE note, 
     calculates its distance from the hit zone:
       float dist = abs(noteY[i] - hitZoneY);
     Tracks the note with the SMALLEST distance (closestDist) and its 
     index (closestIndex).
     
     WHY THIS APPROACH: Since multiple notes can be on screen 
     simultaneously, this ensures the tap is always evaluated against 
     the MOST RELEVANT note (the one nearest the hit zone), avoiding 
     ambiguity when several notes exist at once.

  B) IF NO ACTIVE NOTES EXIST AT ALL (closestIndex == -1):
     - feedbackText = "EARLY!" (player tapped with nothing on screen to hit)
     - feedbackTime = millis()
     - Function returns immediately (no score change, no miss registered 
       - this is a "free" false tap with only a text warning, not 
       penalized as a miss)

  C) SCORING TIERS BASED ON closestDist:
     - IF closestDist <= 3 (very close to perfect center alignment):
         PERFECT: score += 10, combo++, feedbackText = "PERFECT!"
     - ELSE IF closestDist <= 8 (reasonably close):
         GOOD: score += 5, combo++, feedbackText = "GOOD"
     - ELSE (too far from the hit zone, even though a note exists):
         MISS: registerMiss() called (increments misses, resets combo), 
         feedbackText = "MISS", function returns immediately (skips the 
         remaining combo/level-up logic below since this path returns 
         early)

  D) ON SUCCESSFUL HIT (PERFECT or GOOD only - MISS returns early above):
     - feedbackTime = millis()
     - noteActive[closestIndex] = false (removes the successfully-hit 
       note from the track)
     - IF combo > maxCombo: maxCombo = combo (track personal best combo)
     - DIFFICULTY PROGRESSION: every 8 combo points (combo % 8 == 0):
         - level++
         - noteSpeed += 0.3 (notes fall faster)
         - IF spawnInterval > 500: spawnInterval -= 100 (notes spawn more 
           frequently, floor of 500ms to prevent overwhelming spawn rates)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.8 void registerMiss()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Centralized miss-handling logic, called both when a note passes 
the hit zone untapped (from updateGame()) and when a mistimed tap occurs 
(from handleTap()).

WHAT IT DOES:
  - misses++ (increment total miss count)
  - combo = 0 (reset current combo streak to zero)
  - feedbackText = "MISS"
  - feedbackTime = millis()

DESIGN NOTE: Having this logic in a single reusable function (rather 
than duplicated in both calling locations) is good practice - ensures 
consistent miss-handling behavior regardless of WHICH situation 
triggered the miss.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.9 void drawGame()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Renders the current frame during active gameplay.

DRAWING ORDER:
  1. Clear display buffer
  2. Draw track boundaries: two vertical lines marking the left and 
     right edges of the note-falling track (at trackX and trackX + 
     trackWidth)
  3. Draw hit zone: a hollow rectangle outline at the target position 
     (trackX, hitZoneY, trackWidth, hitZoneHeight)
  4. Draw all ACTIVE notes: each rendered as a filled rectangle slightly 
     narrower than the track (trackWidth - 4, with 2px margin on each 
     side) at its current noteY position (cast to int for pixel-based 
     drawing since noteY is stored as float)
  5. Draw feedback text (if currently active) positioned just above the 
     hit zone
  6. Draw HUD elements:
     - LEFT SIDE: "Score:" label + numeric value, "Lvl:" + level value
     - RIGHT SIDE: "Combo" label + numeric value, "Miss:X/5" counter
  7. Push frame to physical display

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.10 void drawStartScreen()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Title/instructions screen shown before gameplay begins.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "RHYTHM TAP" title
  - "Tap when note hits" / "the box at bottom!" instructions
  - "Perfect timing = more" / "points. Good luck!" scoring hint

NOTE: Includes a small delay(50) at the end - a minor stability delay 
that, while technically blocking, is brief enough to not affect button 
responsiveness on the start screen meaningfully.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.11 void drawGameOver()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Final results screen shown after reaching the miss limit.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "GAME OVER"
  - "Score: [final score]"
  - "Max Combo: [best combo this game]"
  - "Best Score: [all-time session best]"
  - "Press to Play Again"

