7. KEY TECHNICAL CONCEPTS DEMONSTRATED
================================================================================

1. MULTIPLE SIMULTANEOUS GAME OBJECTS (OBJECT POOLING PATTERN)
   Unlike Flappy Bird's fixed 3-pipe array (always active) or Stop The 
   Bar's single bar, Rhythm Tap manages up to 6 independent notes using 
   an "object pool" pattern - a fixed-size array where slots are marked 
   active/inactive and reused, avoiding dynamic memory allocation 
   entirely (critical for Arduino's limited RAM).

2. FLOAT-BASED POSITION TRACKING FOR SMOOTH SUB-PIXEL MOVEMENT
   noteY[] uses float instead of int, allowing noteSpeed values like 1.2 
   pixels/frame to accumulate smoothly over time rather than being 
   truncated/rounded each frame - resulting in smoother-looking motion 
   at fractional speeds.

3. "CLOSEST MATCH" INPUT RESOLUTION
   When multiple notes exist on screen simultaneously, handleTap() 
   doesn't just check the FIRST or LAST note - it actively searches for 
   the CLOSEST one to the hit zone, ensuring the game always interprets 
   player input against the most contextually relevant target.

4. TIERED DISTANCE-BASED SCORING
   Similar in concept to Stop The Bar's proximity scoring, but 
   implemented with discrete tiers (PERFECT/GOOD/MISS) rather than a 
   continuous formula - demonstrating an alternative scoring design 
   pattern for timing-based games.

5. COMBO-DRIVEN DIFFICULTY SCALING
   Like Stop The Bar's streak-based difficulty, Rhythm Tap increases 
   speed and spawn rate based on combo milestones (every 8 points) 
   rather than total score or elapsed time - rewarding/challenging 
   skilled, consistent players more aggressively.

6. DUAL-CONDITION MISS DETECTION
   Misses can occur in TWO distinct ways in this game: (1) a note 
   passing the hit zone completely untapped, or (2) a mistimed tap when 
   a note exists but isn't close enough. Both paths funnel into the 
   same registerMiss() function, demonstrating good code reuse for 
   shared failure-state logic.

7. TIME-LIMITED UI FEEDBACK MESSAGES
   feedbackText combined with feedbackTime and feedbackDuration 
   implements a simple non-blocking "toast message" system - text 
   appears, then automatically disappears after a fixed duration, 
   without ever using delay() to pause gameplay.

